this week brought very conflicting  signals on the state of the u.s economy  on friday the government's employment  report for the month of april saw a  better than expected gain of almost 430  000 jobs with unemployment remaining at  3.6 percent but friday also closed out a  volatile week on wall street three major  indices lost ground for the week for the  s p 500 it was the fifth losing week in  a row the longest streak in more than a  decade joining us to talk all about it  is cbs news senior business analyst jill  schlesinger jill good morning and what  can you tell us about this jobs report  you know this was a solid report as you  said slightly better than expected on  job creation rate stays near those  50-year lows just above and about but  overall in the last year we have  actually seen 6.6 million jobs created  in the u.s economy it's a very strong  number and if you think about it this is  amazing because we had lost 22 million  in the spring of 2020 and now we've  recovered 95  of those jobs so that's really good on  the wage front we saw wages up by five  and a half percent sounds good but as we  know that leaves a lot of americans  short with inflation running at about  eight and a half percent annually  jill it was a wild week uh for the  markets um some big swings which it  panicked i think a lot of people where  do you think the markets are right now  and what should investors be thinking  you know i think everyone is really  focused on the federal reserve we had a  federal reserve meeting this week they  raised interest rates by a half of a  percentage point that was the biggest  increase in more than 20 years and it  looks like what the fed is really trying  to do is delicately balance the u.s  economy they don't want to raise rates  by so much that we actually end up  slowing down and going into a recession  but they've got to keep raising rates  because inflation remains so high so  think of it this way they're driving a  car it's speeding down the highway and  right now if they slam the brakes on we  end up in a recession ditch if they just  tap the brakes too lightly we keep  speeding along and that is a tough job  for the fed  jill you know people have retirement  accounts they're concerned about you say  this to us every week on the call if  your money's there you don't need it  just keep it there  if you need to retire it's a little more  worrisome what about even just savers  and  borrowers right now what do they need to  worry about well  so let's look at three different  categories if you're an investor and  you're long term you're right you've got  to kind of keep on keeping on that's  what you do if you are a saver and  interest rates are rising here's  something really good you could start  seeing higher interest rates credited in  your savings and checking and money  market accounts now those go very slowly  on the way up right and that's very  difficult if you're a saver if you're a  borrower of course you're going to see  higher rates on credit cards on auto  loans on those adjustable rate mortgages  some small business loans and i think  that the the struggle here as a consumer  is that you're trying to figure out what  do i do with my money and i think the  reality is if you're a borrower things  cost more if you're a saver you're  earning a little bit of interest but not  enough to keep pace with inflation one  interesting idea to consider for people  who are really freaked out about  inflation and have some money the  government has  inflation-linked savings bonds they're  called i-bonds and those bonds right now  are using a  two parts of of the bond and you get a  total interest rate of  9.62 percent between now and october  you've got to hold those bonds from one  to five years it's only ten thousand  dollars maximum that you can put in for  each calendar year but if you're worried  that's not a terrible place to park some  money  very interesting idea jill thank you  very much as always
 

